<!-- ============================================================
     Fortinet FortiSandbox JRPC API OS Command Injection
     CVE-2026-39808 (CWE-78: OS Command Injection)
     Affected: FortiSandbox prior to 5.0.6 and 4.4.9
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1059.004 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Unix Shell (TA0002 Execution)
       T1562.001 - Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools (TA0005)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-16, Federal Deadline 2026-07-19
     Rule IDs: 103986-103992
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-16

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-39808 is a critical OS command injection vulnerability (CVSS
     9.1-9.8) in the Fortinet FortiSandbox JRPC API. An unauthenticated
     network attacker can send a crafted HTTP request to the JRPC API endpoint
     with malicious parameters injected with OS command metacharacters. The
     JRPC handler passes these parameters unsanitized to a system() or
     equivalent OS function call, resulting in arbitrary OS command execution
     under the FortiSandbox web service process identity (typically root or
     a privileged service account).

     FortiSandbox is the threat intelligence backbone for other Fortinet
     security products (FortiGate, FortiMail, FortiWeb, FortiClient).
     Compromise of FortiSandbox can silently disable threat verdicts
     across an organization's entire Fortinet security stack, creating
     organization-wide blind spots.

     CVE-2026-39808 was actively exploited from June 2026 per KEVIntel sensors.
     CISA added it to the KEV catalog on 2026-07-16 with a July 19 federal deadline.

     NOTE: CVE-2026-25089 (a separate FortiSandbox OS command injection affecting
     the Web UI VNC start endpoint) was addressed in prior detection PR #41 (merged
     2026-06-25). CVE-2026-39808 targets a DISTINCT endpoint (JRPC API) and
     requires separate detection rules. Both CVEs may be exploited in the same
     campaign; operators should treat alerts from either rule set as related signals.

     Attack chain:
       1. Attacker sends an HTTP POST (or GET with parameters) to the FortiSandbox
          JRPC API endpoint (/jrpc or equivalent path prefix) with a payload
          containing OS command injection metacharacters (semicolon, pipe, &&,
          backtick, dollar-parenthesis) in one or more parameters.
       2. The JRPC request handler passes the unsanitized parameter value to
          an OS command execution function.
       3. Attacker achieves arbitrary OS command execution as the web service
          process identity (typically root on FortiSandbox appliance).
       4. Follow-on impact: deployment of persistent backdoor, credential
          harvesting, or disabling FortiSandbox threat verdict processing to
          blind downstream Fortinet enforcement products.

     Detection Approach:

     HTTP / Traefik reverse-proxy layer (rules 103986-103991):
       Rule 103986 (level 7): Base anchor - any HTTP request to the FortiSandbox
         JRPC API endpoint path. FortiSandbox management interfaces typically use
         /jrpc, /api/v1/jrpc, or /api/v2/jrpc paths. This anchor captures all
         traffic to these endpoints for correlation with child rules.
       Rule 103987 (level 11): POST to FortiSandbox JRPC API (primary exploit
         delivery method). Crafted JSON or form-encoded parameters containing
         injection metacharacters are delivered via POST body.
       Rule 103988 (level 13): POST to FortiSandbox JRPC returning HTTP 2xx
         (server processed the unauthenticated request). A successful response
         to an unauthenticated crafted POST indicates the request was accepted
         and the JRPC handler processed the parameters, including any injected
         OS commands.
       Rule 103989 (level 14): Any request to FortiSandbox JRPC with command
         injection metacharacters detected in the URL path or query string.
         URL-encoded variants (%3B=semicolon, %7C=pipe, %60=backtick) are
         included; attackers frequently URL-encode metacharacters to bypass
         simple string filters.
       Rule 103990 (level 15): POST to FortiSandbox JRPC returning 2xx with
         command injection metacharacters in the URL (highest-confidence signal).
         Both the successful processing and the injection pattern are present;
         confirms active CVE-2026-39808 exploitation.
       Rule 103991 (level 9): Probe burst - 8 or more requests from the same
         source IP to FortiSandbox JRPC within 60 seconds. Automated exploit
         tooling iterates parameters and payloads rapidly; this burst pattern
         has no legitimate operational explanation.

     Syslog / host process layer (rule 103992):
       Rule 103992 (level 14): Syslog from FortiSandbox indicating unexpected
         process execution by the web service daemon. Successful CVE-2026-39808
         exploitation causes the JRPC handler (running as httpd, python, or a
         custom daemon) to spawn OS commands. FortiSandbox syslog typically
         captures unexpected execve() calls or shell invocations as system
         log entries. Pattern matches common post-exploit shell commands.

     Log source prerequisites:
       HTTP layer:
         Traefik access logs in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
         Field names: RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost, DownstreamStatus,
         ClientHost. FortiSandbox management interface must be behind Traefik.
       Syslog layer:
         FortiSandbox appliance syslog forwarded to Wazuh via syslog input or
         Wazuh agent. The FortiSandbox daemon process name typically appears as
         httpd, python, fortisandbox, or fdsm in syslog process fields.

     Severity tiers:
        7 - Any request to FortiSandbox JRPC endpoint (info anchor)
       11 - POST to FortiSandbox JRPC (exploit delivery method)
       13 - POST to JRPC returning 2xx (unauthenticated request processed)
       14 - JRPC request with command injection metacharacters in URL
       15 - POST to JRPC returning 2xx + injection metacharacters (confirmed exploit)
        9 - Probe burst: 8+ requests from same IP to JRPC in 60 seconds
       14 - Syslog: FortiSandbox web process spawning shell commands (post-exploit)

     False positive guidance:
       103986: FortiSandbox legitimate management API calls from the Fortinet
         management console originate from known internal IPs; baseline these
         and suppress via Wazuh overrides. External/internet-sourced requests
         to the JRPC API have no legitimate business justification.
       103987: Authorized FortiSandbox REST/JRPC API clients (Fabric integration,
         EMS, FortiManager) use authenticated POST; review ClientHost against
         the authorized integration IP allowlist.
       103988: A 2xx response to an authenticated API POST from a known integration
         is expected; flag responses to requests from unknown or external IPs.
       103989: URL-encoded semicolons or pipes in legitimate FortiSandbox API
         parameters are unusual; review the full URL and request context. If
         the source IP matches an authorized management system, escalate for
         manual review rather than suppressing.
       103990: No legitimate FortiSandbox API integration sends command injection
         metacharacters and receives a 2xx response; treat as confirmed exploitation
         requiring immediate incident response and FortiSandbox isolation.
       103991: Legitimate Fortinet Fabric integrations poll FortiSandbox APIs on
         a schedule but do not generate rapid burst traffic; bursts indicate
         automated scanning or exploit tool activity.
       103992: FortiSandbox system processes occasionally spawn shell commands
         for legitimate administrative tasks; correlate with HTTP-layer alerts
         (103988 or 103990) from the same timeframe to confirm exploitation.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,execution,exploit_pubfacing,fortisandbox,cisa_kev,cve_2026_39808,os_command_injection,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs)
       Rule IDs: 103986-103991

       CVE-2026-39808 is exploited by sending crafted HTTP requests to
       the FortiSandbox JRPC API with OS command injection metacharacters
       in parameters. Monitoring the reverse proxy layer catches exploit
       delivery based on endpoint access, POST method, response codes,
       and URL-embedded injection patterns.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: any HTTP request to the FortiSandbox JRPC API endpoint.

       The FortiSandbox JRPC API is accessible at path prefixes including
       /jrpc, /api/v1/jrpc, and /api/v2/jrpc. Under CVE-2026-39808, an
       unauthenticated attacker sends a crafted request to this API with
       injected OS command metacharacters in request parameters or JSON body.

       This anchor is level 7 (informational correlation only). All HTTP-layer
       child rules (103987-103991) chain from this anchor. Any external or
       unexpected source accessing FortiSandbox JRPC endpoints should be
       investigated against the authorized management IP allowlist. -->
  <rule id="103986" level="7">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/(?:api/v[12]/)?jrpc\b</field>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808: Request to FortiSandbox JRPC API endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- CVE-2026-39808 OS command injection attack surface anchor; correlate with child rules 103987-103992 (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,jrpc_api,initial_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST method to FortiSandbox JRPC API: elevated suspicion.

       CVE-2026-39808 is primarily exploited via HTTP POST with a crafted
       JSON body containing OS command injection metacharacters in parameter
       values. GET requests with injection in query strings are also possible
       but less common for this JRPC attack path. POST to FortiSandbox JRPC
       from any non-authorized source IP, or from any external IP, should
       trigger investigation. Authorized FortiSandbox API integrations
       (FortiManager, EMS, Fabric) originate from known, allowlisted internal
       IP ranges. -->
  <rule id="103987" level="11">
    <if_sid>103986</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808: POST to FortiSandbox JRPC API $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- POST is the primary exploit delivery method for CVE-2026-39808 JRPC OS command injection; review source IP against authorized Fortinet integration allowlist (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,jrpc_api,post_method,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to FortiSandbox JRPC returning HTTP 2xx: unauthenticated request processed.

       Under a correctly patched or protected FortiSandbox instance, unauthenticated
       requests to the JRPC API return HTTP 401 (Unauthorized) or 403 (Forbidden).
       A 2xx response status indicates the JRPC handler accepted and processed the
       unauthenticated request, confirming the CVE-2026-39808 vulnerability is
       present and that any injected OS commands in the request body were passed
       to the OS command execution function.

       This is a high-confidence exploitation signal. Capture source IP, timestamp,
       request body size, and response body for incident response. -->
  <rule id="103988" level="13">
    <if_sid>103987</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808: POST to FortiSandbox JRPC API $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) at $(RequestHost) -- unauthenticated JRPC request accepted and processed; CVE-2026-39808 vulnerability confirmed; injected OS commands may have executed (T1190/T1059.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,jrpc_api,post_method,auth_bypass,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Request to FortiSandbox JRPC with OS command injection metacharacters in URL.

       When CVE-2026-39808 payloads are delivered via URL query parameters
       (rather than POST body), the injection metacharacters appear in the
       RequestPath field of Traefik access logs. Common metacharacters used
       in OS command injection:
         - %3B / semicolon (;): command separator
         - %7C / pipe (|): command piping
         - %60 / backtick (`): command substitution
         - %26%26 / &&: conditional command execution
         - %24%28 / dollar-paren ($(...)): command substitution
         - %0A / newline (\n): command separator bypass
       This rule fires on both URL-encoded and raw metacharacter forms
       detected in the RequestPath field. -->
  <rule id="103989" level="14">
    <if_sid>103986</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:%3B|%7C|%60|%26%26|%0[aA]|%24%28|;[a-z]|\|[a-z]|`[a-z]|\$\()</field>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808: Request to FortiSandbox JRPC API $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) contains OS command injection metacharacters in URL -- active CVE-2026-39808 exploitation attempt; URL contains shell metacharacters indicating command injection payload (T1190/T1059.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,jrpc_api,command_injection,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to FortiSandbox JRPC returning 2xx + command injection in URL: RCE confirmed.

       Highest-confidence signal: both the successful HTTP response (confirming the
       JRPC handler processed the request) and the OS command injection metacharacters
       in the URL path are present in a single event. This combination means an
       unauthenticated request containing injection metacharacters was accepted and
       processed by the FortiSandbox JRPC handler, confirming active exploitation
       of CVE-2026-39808. Initiate immediate incident response. -->
  <rule id="103990" level="15">
    <if_sid>103989</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808 CRITICAL: POST to FortiSandbox JRPC $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) with command injection metacharacters -- confirmed active CVE-2026-39808 OS command injection exploitation; OS commands have executed; initiate incident response and isolate FortiSandbox immediately (T1190/T1059.004/T1562.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,jrpc_api,command_injection,rce_confirmed,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Probe burst: 8 or more requests from same IP to FortiSandbox JRPC in 60 seconds.

       Automated CVE-2026-39808 exploitation scripts (including AI-assisted exploit
       generators observed in the wild as of June 2026) iterate over multiple
       parameter positions, injection metacharacters, and encoding variations in
       rapid succession. A burst of 8 or more requests from a single source IP
       to FortiSandbox JRPC endpoints within 60 seconds is a strong scanner or
       exploit tool fingerprint with no legitimate operational explanation.

       Related: CVE-2026-25089 (Web UI VNC endpoint) was observed exploited by
       AI-generated exploit attempts in June 2026; similar automated probing
       patterns are expected for CVE-2026-39808. -->
  <rule id="103991" level="9" frequency="8" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103986</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808: Probe burst - $(ClientHost) sent 8+ requests to FortiSandbox JRPC API at $(RequestHost) within 60 seconds -- automated exploit tooling or scanner fingerprint; investigate source IP for CVE-2026-39808 exploitation activity (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,jrpc_api,probe_burst,scanner,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: FortiSandbox syslog - host process execution
       Rule ID: 103992

       Successful CVE-2026-39808 exploitation causes the FortiSandbox JRPC
       handler process to spawn OS commands. FortiSandbox appliances forward
       syslog to Wazuh. This rule detects unexpected shell command patterns
       in syslog entries attributable to FortiSandbox web service processes
       (httpd, python, fortisandbox, fdsm).

       Correlate with HTTP-layer alerts (103988 or 103990) from the same
       timeframe to confirm CVE-2026-39808 exploitation rather than
       legitimate administrative shell use.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Syslog: FortiSandbox web service process executing suspicious OS commands.

       Following CVE-2026-39808 exploitation, common attacker follow-on OS
       commands observed in the wild include:
         - id, whoami: identity confirmation
         - wget, curl: payload download from attacker C2
         - nc, ncat, bash -i: reverse shell establishment
         - python -c, perl -e: inline interpreter for reverse shell
         - crontab, at: persistence establishment
         - rm -rf / chmod 777: destructive or access-enabling actions
       FortiSandbox syslog entries from the web service process (httpd or
       fortisandbox daemon) that contain these command strings are a
       strong indicator of post-exploitation activity. -->
  <rule id="103992" level="14">
    <decoded_as>syslog</decoded_as>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)(?:httpd|python[23]?|fortisandbox|fdsm)\[.*(?:wget\s+http|curl\s+-[a-z]*\s+http|nc\s+-[a-z]*\s+\d|bash\s+-i|python\s+-c|perl\s+-e|/bin/sh\s+-c|crontab\s+-[el]|chmod\s+[0-7]{3,4}\s+/|id\s*;|whoami\s*;|cat\s+/etc/(?:passwd|shadow)|uname\s+-[ar])</regex>
    <description>FortiSandbox CVE-2026-39808: Suspicious OS command in FortiSandbox web service process syslog on $(agent.name) -- web service process executed shell command consistent with post-CVE-2026-39808 exploitation; correlate with HTTP-layer events for confirmation (T1059.004/T1562.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>fortisandbox,cve_2026_39808,execution,post_exploit,command_injection,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
